---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../config";

const facts = [
    {
        label: "Ester",
        line: "Mountain guide & mother",
        text: "Always learning",
        country: "Tanzania",
    },
    {
        label: "Radoslav",
        line: "Developer & psychologist",
        text: "Always learning",
        country: "Bulgaria",
    },  
];
---

<Layout
    title="About"
    description={`About ${site.name} — Mountaineering and Technology.`}
>
    <article class="page prose">
        <h1 class="page__title">Peace Initiative</h1>

        <p class="lede">
            <i>This is a personal, non-political initiative.<br /> Together with my 
            partner, a citizen of Tanzania, I have written to the Ukrainian and 
            Russian embassies in Sofia and to Bulgaria’s Ministry of Foreign Affairs. 
            Our purpose is not to represent any government or take a side in the conflict, 
            but to encourage humanitarian contact, dialogue, and a peaceful, 
            negotiated end to the war.</i>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            Status of the letter: <b>Sent to the Embassy of Ukraine, the Embassy of Russia, 
            and the Bulgarian Ministry of Foreign Affairs on 21 November 2026.</b>
        </p>
        
        <p>
            The letter:
        </p>
        
        <p>Dear Excellency,</p>

        <p>
            I am writing to you as a private citizen of Bulgaria, together with my partner, 
            who is a citizen of Tanzania. We are writing in a spirit of complete neutrality 
            and goodwill, with no political affiliation and no mandate from any government 
            or organisation.
        </p>

        <p>
            Our sole purpose is to express our deep concern about the continuing human, 
            economic, and geopolitical cost of the war between Russia and Ukraine, and to 
            encourage both sides to consider every possible path toward a ceasefire, negotiations, 
            and a durable peace.
        </p>

        <p>
           We recognise that this conflict involves profound questions of security, sovereignty, 
           international law, territorial integrity, and historical grievance. We do not seek to 
           minimise the suffering of either side, nor to prejudge any final political settlement. 
           In particular, a visit to Kyiv or Moscow by us would in no way constitute recognition 
           of Crimea or any other territory as belonging to either side. It would be solely a 
           humanitarian and peace-oriented gesture, intended to show that private citizens can 
           stand for dialogue even when official negotiations are difficult. 
        </p>

        <p>
            There are also strong practical reasons to seek an end to the fighting.
        </p>

        <p>
            Ukraine’s economic situation remains extremely difficult. The country 
            depends heavily on financial assistance from the European Union and other 
            international partners to sustain public services, reconstruction, defence, 
            and its broader economy. The EU has established major support mechanisms, 
            including a €90 billion Ukraine Support Loan for 2026–2027, while further 
            financing proposals have been discussed to address Ukraine’s continuing needs. 
            Continued war therefore places a heavy and prolonged burden not only on Ukraine, 
            but also on its partners and taxpayers.
        </p>

        <p>
           Russia, too, has paid an enormous price. The war has required major direct expenditure, 
           disrupted trade and investment, and contributed to long-term economic and financial 
           costs. In addition, Russian state assets remain immobilised in Europe. 
           Estimates place frozen Russian central-bank assets at around €300 billion globally, 
           with approximately €210 billion in the EU, largely held at Euroclear in Belgium. 
           These assets, and the income generated from them, have become central to discussions about 
           financing Ukraine’s recovery and compensation. Their continued immobilisation represents a 
           major long-term financial and strategic loss for Russia, which could only be resolved 
           through a settlement that ends the conflict and addresses compensation and legal questions.
        </p>

        <p>
           Neither side can realistically expect a military solution to produce a stable, prosperous, 
           and secure future. Every additional month of war brings further casualties, displacement, 
           destruction of infrastructure, separation of families, and deepening hostility between 
           societies that will have to coexist in Europe for generations. 
        </p>
        
	<p>
            We respectfully appeal to both governments to consider:
        </p>

        <p>
            1. An immediate, verifiable ceasefire and protection of civilians.
        </p>

        <p>
            2. Humanitarian access, prisoner exchanges, and the safe return of displaced people 
            where possible.
        </p>
        
        <p>
            3. A serious negotiating process, including international guarantees and security 
            arrangements acceptable to both sides.
        </p>

        <p>
            4. A settlement that respects international law, the sovereignty and territorial integrity of 
            Ukraine, and the legitimate security concerns of all parties.
        </p>

        <p>
            5. A framework for reconstruction, economic recovery, and the eventual resolution of 
            frozen-asset and compensation issues.
        </p>
        
        <p>
            6. A long-term vision in which Ukraine and Russia can live in peace, without permanent hostility 
            or the threat of renewed war.
        </p>
        
        <p>
            We understand that peace cannot be built on unrealistic expectations or on terms that either 
            side regards as unjust. But we believe that diplomacy, patience, and direct communication 
            remain preferable to continued destruction. Even a modest private initiative, if it encourages 
            dialogue and human contact, may be worth undertaking.
        </p>
        
        <p>
            We would be grateful if Your Excellency would consider receiving us, or forwarding our appeal 
            to the appropriate officials in Kyiv or Moscow. We would be willing to travel to either capital 
            solely for peaceful, humanitarian, and dialogue-oriented purposes.
        </p>
        
        <p>
            We remain entirely neutral in this conflict. Our aim is not to support one side against the other, 
            but to support life, peace, and the possibility of a negotiated future.
        </p>
        
        <p>Respectfully,</p>
        

        <div class="facts">
            {
                facts.map((f) => (
                    <section class="fact">
                        <h2 class="fact__label">{f.label}</h2>
                        <p class="fact__line">{f.line}</p>
                        <p class="fact__line">{f.text}</p>
                        <p class="fact__muted">{f.country}</p>
                    </section>
                ))
            }
        </div>
    </article>
</Layout>
